   "source": [
    "# Network Flow Models for Transit Planning\n",
    "\n",
    "This project analyzes a simplified Vancouver transit network using linear optimization techniques.\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is to compare how different network designs affect passenger flow and routing cost, while showing how optimization can support transit-planning decisions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "43034476-e8b5-4c69-aee8-302e5a58b45b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Setup\n",
    "\n",
    "The notebook uses helper functions from the `src/` folder to keep the main analysis focused on the models and results."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2dba9194-e2e4-4a79-9b8a-6cabdc56f4d9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "from IPython.display import Image, display\n",
    "\n",
    "from src.solve import run_glpk, print_glpk_block\n",
    "from src.plot import plot_flow_network, plot_multicommodity_flow\n",
    "\n",
    "nodes = pd.read_csv(\"data/nodes.txt\", sep=r\"\\s+\", engine=\"python\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e320d2f3-78ff-40cf-a624-4d10eae8f3fb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Network Representation\n",
    "\n",
    "The transit system is represented as a directed network.\n",
    "\n",
    "Nodes represent major locations in the Vancouver area, while directed edges represent transportation corridors between locations. The network contains 25 locations positioned using longitude and latitude coordinate data stored in `data/nodes.txt`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "56658f14-a63c-4acb-957b-c40651aacc98",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Image object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {
      "image/png": {
       "width": 1200
      }
     },
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "display(Image(filename=\"data/Vancouver_graph.png\", width=1200))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e413b32f-464c-4b62-8260-d67088a7ed8a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The modeled transit system consists of 13 bus routes connecting different regions of the city. Each route has a sequence of corridors it serves, along with a service frequency and passenger load limit.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, Route `16` serves the corridor sequence\n",
    "\n",
    "`BoundaryHastings — HastingsClark — Downtown`.\n",
    "\n",
    "With a service frequency of 6 buses per hour and a passenger load limit of 50 passengers per bus, Route `16` contributes 300 passengers per hour of modeled capacity to each corridor in the sequence.\n",
    "\n",
    "Passenger flow is measured as an hourly rate of riders moving through the network. Corridor capacity represents the maximum passenger flow that the modeled bus service can support along a connection.\n",
    "\n",
    "When more than one route serves the same corridor, their capacities are added together. For instance, Routes `14` and `16` both serve the connection between `BoundaryHastings` and `HastingsClark`, so that corridor receives capacity from both routes.\n",
    "\n",
    "The optimization models use corridor capacities to limit how much passenger flow can move through each connection. In practical terms, passengers can move through the network by transferring at shared locations, but transfer wait times and specific bus schedules are outside the scope of the models."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7121f780-5d7c-45dd-ab51-e51bb410d566",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Baseline Maximum-Flow Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "The baseline analysis estimates how much passenger flow the existing transit system can support.\n",
    "\n",
    "Several boundary locations are treated as passenger sources, while `Downtown` and `UBC` are treated as destinations. The objective is to maximize total passenger flow from the source nodes to the destination nodes while respecting corridor capacities."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "5eb99825-8109-499a-9c07-42a4b148e7fc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "OPTIMAL LP SOLUTION FOUND\n",
      "Wrote solution to outputs/sol_maxflow_baseline.txt\n",
      "Model has been successfully processed\n",
      "SOURCE flow:\n",
      "     MarineGranville           -1400\n",
      "           MarineOak            -300\n",
      "        MarineCambie            -200\n",
      "        KnightMarine            -600\n",
      "      MarineBoundary              -0\n",
      "    KingswayBoundary            -900\n",
      "    LougheedBoundary            -600\n",
      "         Boundary1st              -0\n",
      "    BoundaryHastings            -600\n",
      "SINK flow:\n",
      "            Downtown            2900\n",
      "                 UBC            1700\n",
      "Total flow: 4600 passengers/hour\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "result = run_glpk(\n",
    "    \"models/maxflow.mod\",\n",
    "    \"data/maxflow_baseline.dat\"\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print_glpk_block(\n",
    "    result,\n",
    "    start_label=\"SOURCE flow:\",\n",
    "    end_label=\"Total flow:\"\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7533c767-2a97-47ee-b58c-611cd5fa4048",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The maximum-flow solution moves **4,600 passengers per hour** from the source nodes to the destination nodes. Most of this flow is directed toward `Downtown`, while a smaller portion is routed toward `UBC`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Flow values for the individual corridors are saved in `outputs/sol_maxflow_baseline.txt`. The plot below shows how this optimal flow is distributed through the network."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "5f15a3b6-f603-4fa2-9d6b-28fb6113c033",
   "metadata": {},
