   "source": [
    "The updated dual values suggest that the new `MarineGranville` to `Marine41st` connection remains the most valuable corridor to add capacity. Additional negative dual values point to other corridors where further network improvements could be considered."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2c96b87f-91f1-4dcb-be60-23f1aa0935c0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Conclusions\n",
    "\n",
    "This project uses several linear optimization models to study passenger movement through a simplified Vancouver transit network. The maximum-flow models estimate how much passenger flow the network can support and show how new transit infrastructure increases downtown-bound capacity. The minimum-cost models shift the focus to efficient routing under fixed passenger-demand assumptions.\n",
    "\n",
    "The multi-destination model extends the analysis by routing `Downtown`-bound and `UBC`-bound passengers simultaneously. Because both groups share the same corridor capacities, the model also supports sensitivity analysis. The dual values identified where added capacity would have the greatest effect, leading to a targeted route modification between `MarineGranville` and `Marine41st`.\n",
    "\n",
    "This modification reduced total transportation cost from approximately **\\$10,826 per hour** to approximately **\\$10,655 per hour**, a savings of about **\\$170 per hour**. More importantly, it illustrates how optimization models can be used to answer complex routing questions and identify promising changes to the network.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the project demonstrates how network modeling, optimization, and flow plots can work together to support transit-planning decisions. By comparing scenarios, estimating costs, and identifying targeted improvements, these tools provide a practical framework for evaluating infrastructure and route-design choices before they are implemented."
   ]
  },
